ntitation is going to be even  more valuable and then finally pathomix  approaches deeper characterizations of  these cell subtypes uh through either  rna-seq or dna or or barcoding or a  variety of other sort of  higher plex applications to to learn a  bit more about some of the players in  this tissue would all be i think of  significant interest  conclusions and acknowledgments we've  covered a lot of ground here and i hope  that  you found most of it comprehensible and  interesting  in terms of take-home points i on a  general level i think this talk really  focused on improving post-transplant  care it also  talked about utilizing modern tools to  maximally extract information from  underutilized resources and that's a lot  of of what  sort of drew me to these methodologies  yes i'm a transplant doctor and  interested in that but i'm  also interested in finding ways to make  the most out of  the data and the resources already  obtained and already available to us  um histologic analysis is  perhaps getting forgotten about in 21st  century medicine a little bit it's  it's not some relic of a bygone era in  medicine in my opinion  there's a wealth of information  contained within these histologic  samples and  and modern software approaches can  really help us maximally get at that  information  and then to share it and ultimately to  integrate it with the rest of the 21st  century diagnostic toolkit  heart transplantation medicine is a  tissue-rich field with  major unmet needs and tissue analysis  and it really makes it ideally suited  for demonstrating the translational  potential of some of these methods  i hope that the research covered in this  presentation has helped to highlight  some of this potential and i thank you  for your attention  a few acknowledgements real quick uh my  close research  collaborators ken marley's and mike  feldman the team at uh coyote  biosciences bethany cliff  and the rest of them for all their help  with this project and uh my